            <ArticleTitle>Efficacy and safety of a novel disposable intravaginal device for treating stress urinary incontinence.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>594.e1-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ajog.2008.01.061</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of this study was to evaluate the efficacy and safety of a novel disposable intravaginal device for treatment of stress urinary incontinence (SUI) in women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Sixty women with severe SUI were recruited from 2 sites in Israel to wear preweighed pads during a 7-day control period followed by a 28-day device usage period in which the device and preweighed pads were worn daily for 8 hours. The primary endpoint was the percentage of women who achieved a &gt; or = 70% reduction in pad weight gain (PWG) from the control period to the last 14 days of device usage.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Sixty women who enrolled into the study and used the device for any period of time were included in the intent to treat (ITT) population. Eighty-five percent of them achieved a &gt; or = 70% reduction in PWG (P = .01). Improvements in overall quality of life, subjective perception of incontinence, and satisfaction with the device were observed.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The intravaginal device is easy to use, well-tolerated, and effective in reducing SUI.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
